+| `want_second_pad(wanted)`, `second_pad()`, `first_pad_connected()`, `first_pad_now()` | A second player's pad, for a local multiplayer mode (`GameProfile::second_player`). While wanted, player 2's device is read into `second_pad()`, never into the game's own pad: the one the Controls page's **Player 2** names (a gamepad, or the keyboard and mouse, which player 1 then does not have), or on Automatic a gamepad other than player 1's. `SecondPad::keyboard` says player 2 is on the keyboard. `first_pad_connected()`: player 1 has a gamepad, not only the keyboard. `first_pad_now()`: player 1's pad sampled at the call. Since interface versions 6 and 7. |
+| `second_player_playing(playing)` | Player 2 is in the game: a gamepad of player 2's then stays player 2's when player 1's goes. Otherwise it moves to player 1, who has none, and player 2 waits for another (a controller swapped on the start menu). `<prefix>_PLAYER2_PAD_STAYS=1` keeps it player 2's always. Since interface version 8, with `SecondPad::keyboard`. |
 | `extra_buttons()` | The extra buttons (`kExtraL2`, `kExtraR2`, `kExtraL3`, `kExtraR3`: the triggers and stick clicks a PSP lacks, or the keys bound to them) held when the game last read its pad. The game never sees them; a port names the ones it uses in `GameProfile::extra_buttons` and gives them a meaning of its own. Since interface version 5. |
 
 Handlers run on the emulation thread, one at a time. A module may include PortableKit's `host/` headers to reach further than this, but those change without notice; a need that comes up twice belongs in the header instead.
diff --git a/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md b/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
index c18ca63..1fc25d7 100644
--- a/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
+++ b/docs/PROFILE_GUIDE.md
@@ -79,6 +79,8 @@ For a picture of the port's own beside one of the game's screens, such as a hint
 
 `sharp_textures` lists pixel art to draw with nearest filtering whatever the Texture filter setting says, for a sprite that smoothing blurs at a higher resolution. Each is a `SharpTexture`: its key as **Dump textures** names the file (the address ignored, xxh64), and its width and height. Only a texture of a listed size is hashed, when it is uploaded, so the list costs nothing for the others. A key covers one texture with one palette, so a sprite sheet loaded with another palette, or as a different part of its picture, has a key of its own: dump each scene it appears in. `<prefix>_NO_SHARP_PIXEL_ART=1` draws them as the setting says, for a comparison. A texture pack's image for a listed texture keeps the pack's own filtering. Without any, the setting decides for every texture.
 
+`second_player` says the port has a local second player (`hle_extension::want_second_pad`): the Controls page then offers **Player 1** and **Player 2**, each Automatic, Keyboard and mouse, or a gamepad by name (`input.player1`, `input.player2`; `<prefix>_PLAYER1`, `<prefix>_PLAYER2`). Without it the page offers one **Controller** row. A gamepad is named by its USB vendor and product ids, so it is the same over USB and Bluetooth, and two alike are one choice that each player can take. On Automatic, player 1 has the keyboard and mouse and the first gamepad free, player 2 the next. A chosen gamepad that is not connected leaves that player on Automatic until it is. The port says when player 2 is in the game (`second_player_playing`); otherwise a gamepad held for player 2 moves to player 1 when player 1's goes.
+
 `edited_textures` lists textures the profile draws again as they load, with `edit_texture` doing the drawing: the game's button symbols in the style of the gamepad in use, for one. Each is an `EditedTexture`, named and found as a `SharpTexture` is. `edit_texture` gets the game's decoded picture (RGBA8) and a `TextureEditContext`, and returns its own picture, the same shape at any size (four times gives room for sharp detail), drawn with the same texture coordinates as a texture pack's image; false keeps the game's. The context holds what the picture may depend on: Button prompts with the gamepad in use and the Confirm button setting (`input::ButtonPrompts`: the style, and which face button sends each of the PSP's), and whether Texture filter is Sharp. When it changes, the cached textures are dropped and the listed ones drawn again. Set `button_prompts` when they are the game's button symbols, and the Controls page offers Button prompts (Automatic, PSP, Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo; Automatic follows the pad, the PSP's with none). A texture pack's image for one is drawn instead. `<prefix>_NO_TEXTURE_EDITS=1` draws the game's own, for a comparison. Keep the game's pixels out of the profile: draw over the picture it is given.
 
 `cheats` lists cheats the port offers on the menu's Cheats page, beside the player's own PPSSPP cheat file (`PSP/Cheats/<disc id>.ini` on the memory stick, `host/cheats/cheats.hpp`). Each is a `BuiltinCheat`: a key for settings.ini, the heading it goes under, a name, a sentence for the footer, and its choices, each a label and its CWCheat code lines (what a cheat file has after `_L`). One choice makes a switch; several make Off and each. They run through the same engine as the file, before it, every 77 ms of the game's time, so a code written as a player would write it gives the same result. A write into recompiled code is left out, as for the file; such a code needs the interpreter, which cheats do not reach yet. Test each against the game's own screens before listing it: a public list's codes can name an address the game no longer reads. Without any, the page has only the switch and the file.
